  "decision": {
    "verdict": "ADOPT the durability generation (group commit)",
    "gate": "barrier amplification = physical barriers / logical fsyncs must fall well below 1 under concurrency; fsync tail latency must not regress; crash court green at every barrier stage",
    "summary": "The group gate coalesces concurrent fsyncs onto ONE physical barrier per generation: amplification 1.00 -> 0.23 at 32 writers (127 physical barriers for 545 logical fsyncs), fsync p99 7.86 -> 4.06 ms (-48%), commit-lock wait 366 -> 13.7 ms (-96%), wall 94 -> 80 ms. The median shifts up at 16/32 writers (a waiter parks for the generation cycle - the brief's explicit trade: a little median for the elimination of the convoy tail). The physical barrier is byte-for-byte unchanged; the crash court injects a crash at every physical-barrier stage under 8 concurrent writers and every RETURNED fsync survives recovery with a clean fsck (returned => recoverable; unreturned => admissible). The unmodified durability, crash_recovery, and io_backend_parity power-loss courts stay green.",
